Sofía came up the fire escape at eight, carrying a bag of tacos and an
expression of professional doom.
    “I was told,” she said, “that this is the secret society. I brought
snacks and my skepticism.” She sat on a crate and unwrapped a taco
with the precision of a coroner. “Talk.”
    We told her. Manny did most of it, badly and affectionately. She
listened without interrupting, which is Sofía’s way of loving you, and
at the end she held up one finger.
    “I believe the speakers. I believe the ledger. I’ve now seen your pho‐
tos of it, and the letterforms are consistent with the eighteenth century,
which I’m furious about. I believe the Calm Index, and I believe nine‐
teen incidents at the bodega and two at the café.” She counted on her
fingers. “I do not believe in the stone. I don’t know what to do with the
stone. So I’m going to put it in a column labeled ‘Unexamined’ and
keep walking. That’s how I work.”
    “That’s fair.”
    “It’s very fair.” She bit the taco. “Here’s my problem. I want to pub‐
lish.”
    “Not yet.”
    “JC. We have a corporation that’s been running a behavioral experi‐
ment on a borough for twenty years, with documents. That’s a story. I
can have it in front of three reporters by Monday.”
    “They know where my grandmother lives.” I said it more roughly
than I meant to. “Seven letters, Sofía. They know where my mother
lives. They know Manny’s name, Doña Luz’s mat. If you publish it,
they don’t stop. They speed up. And the people on the other end of
those letters are seventy-nine.”
    She looked at me for a moment.
    “Seven letters is a paper trail,” she said, quietly. “They wrote them.
They put a signature on them. I love it when people write.” She set the
taco down. “But you don’t get to decide this one for me either.”
    “See?” said Manny, into his taco.
    “Don’t see,” I said.



